    0.0     # b   – bias term (set to zero to avoid a constant drift)
]
```

**Why this might work**

| Parameter | Intended effect (based on typical state ordering) |
|-----------|---------------------------------------------------|
| `w0 = -1.0` | Strong negative feedback on cart‑position error, pulling the cart back toward the centre. |
| `w5 = -5.0` | Aggressive damping on cart‑velocity to curb overshoot. |
| `w1 = 5.0`, `w2 = -5.0` | Opposite‑sign feedback on the two pole angles – a classic strategy for the double‑pole problem (push left when pole 1 leans right, push right when pole 2 leans left). |
| `w3 = 0.5`, `w4 = -0.5` | Moderate correction of the angular velocities of the two poles. |
| `w6`, `w7` (both –2) | Extra negative feedback on the remaining state variables (typically cos/sin components of the pole angles) to stabilise the dynamics further. |
| `w8 = 0.5` | Small positive gain on the final state component (often a velocity‑related term) to give the controller a little “push” when needed. |
| `bias = 0` | No constant offset, so the controller only reacts to the observed error. |

This configuration is inspired by the higher‑performing runs you’ve already tried (e.g., the `[-0.5, 4.5, -4.5, 0.5, -0.5, -0.5, -1, -1, 0.3]` family) but pushes the main feedback gains a bit harder to try and extend the episode length.  

Give this a run and see how many steps you can hold the cart‑pole upright. If the episode still ends early, you can modestly reduce the magnitude of the strongest gains (e.g., change `-5.0` → `-4.0`) or add a small bias (`+0.1` – `+0.2`) to counter any residual drift. Good luck!
